WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.290
Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker has proven
to be a bit divisive among fans - but in an

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.830
alternate universe, they were divided by a
totally different movie.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:13.200
George Lucas had his own plans for the sequel
trilogy before selling Lucasfilm to Disney

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.350
in 2012, and it's safe to say that his conclusion
to the Skywalker Saga would have borne little

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.039
resemblance to the film we got.

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:23.941
A key piece of information we have concerning
Lucas' vision for the ninth episode of the

00:00:23.941 --> 00:00:28.520
main Star Wars series comes from Mark Hamill,
Luke Skywalker himself, in an interview the

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.750
star gave to IGN in March 2018.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.580
Now that Rise of Skywalker has been unleashed
on the world, there is new context for his

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.670
remarks - and it's interesting to take stock
of how the creator's plans shifted due to

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.940
the involvement of new creatives and unforeseen
circumstances.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:47.370
Specifically, Hamill noted that while Lucas
had indeed planned to kill off Luke, he didn't

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:52.090
intend to do so until the end of Episode 9;
you'll recall that Luke essentially sacrificed

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:56.610
himself at the conclusion of Star Wars: The
Last Jedi in order to allow the besieged remains

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:00.450
of the Resistance to escape the First Order
onslaught on Crait.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:04.309
In Lucas' version of the sequel trilogy, though,
Luke would have had another order of business

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:08.830
to attend to in the next film: training his
sister, Leia, in the ways of the Jedi.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:13.400
In his talk with IGN, Hamill lamented that
this narrative thread was, quote, "never played

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.211
upon" in The Last Jedi, even though that film
- the only one of the sequel flicks to be

00:01:17.211 --> 00:01:22.189
co-written and directed by Rian Johnson rather
than J.J. Abrams - was reportedly more in

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:24.750
line with Lucas' vision for the trilogy.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:29.501
Of course, it goes without saying that Fisher's
death in 2016 resulted in Leia's role in The

00:01:29.501 --> 00:01:34.159
Last Jedi and The Rise of Skywalker being
greatly diminished, although given Lucas'

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:38.560
affinity for CGI, it doesn't necessarily follow
that this would have been the case had he

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:39.840
remained in charge.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.750
It's pretty easy to pinpoint some of the other
major ways in which Lucas' sequel trilogy

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:47.549
would have differed from the one we got; for
one thing, they would have centered on completely

00:01:47.549 --> 00:01:52.310
different characters, as Rey, Poe, Finn, and
Kylo Ren were not his creations.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.899
Yes, Lucas did intend to bring back Luke,
Leia, and Han Solo - but in regard to Rise

00:01:56.899 --> 00:02:01.140
of Skywalker in particular, there's one returning
character from that film that would not have

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.280
made the cut in Lucas' version.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.239
This would be Ian McDiarmid's Emperor Palpatine,
whose controversial return was Abrams' idea

00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:12.590
and whose big comeback McDiarmid recently
admitted to being blindsided by, for a simple

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:13.590
reason.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:17.280
Speaking with Digital Spy, the actor revealed
that Lucas had no further plans for the character

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.800
- at least not in terms of the films' in-universe
chronology - after his apparent demise during

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.950
the climax of Star Wars: Return of the Jedi.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:24.950
McDiarmid said:

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:25.950
"I thought I was dead!

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.290
I thought [Palpatine] was dead."

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:31.900
"Because when we did Return of the Jedi, and
I was thrown down that chute to Galactic Hell...

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.290
I said, 'Oh, does he come back?'

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.349
And George said, 'No, he's dead.'"

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:40.720
McDiarmid went on to say that he had accepted
his character's fate, up until the prequels.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.750
"In a sense he wasn't dead, because we went
back to revisit him when he was a young man.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.620
But I was totally surprised by this."

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.590
We can also glean from Lucas' own remarks
that, while the iconic principal characters

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:55.660
from the original trilogy would have played
a major role in his version of the trilogy,

00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:00.720
the focus of the plot would have been... slightly
more esoteric than Disney's.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.250
In 2018, a quote from the companion book to
the AMC docu-series James Cameron's Story

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:09.340
of Science Fiction made the rounds online,
and it revealed that Lucas had at one point

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:12.700
been prepared to take the story in directions
nobody could have foreseen.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:13.700
The director explained:

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:17.220
"[The sequel films] were going to get into
a microbiotic world.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:20.050
There's this world of creatures that operate
differently than we do.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.680
I call them the Whills.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:23.940
And the Whills are the ones who actually control
the universe.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.540
They feed off the Force...

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.190
If I'd held onto the company I could have
done it..."

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:32.299
Given many observers' gripes that the Disney
sequel trilogy suffered from a lack of overall

00:03:32.299 --> 00:03:37.269
cohesive vision, Lucas' version may even have
proven to be more satisfying for the franchise's

00:03:37.269 --> 00:03:38.299
fans.

